Brent J. Nordquist wrote:
> My thanks to this group and those who've written the guides, FAQs,
> spoilers etc. for how much you've already helped me start to succeed at
> nethack. I play Wiz-Elf-Mal-Cha and finally reached XL11 and beat the
> mines and Sokoban this week.
> 
> I have a few things I haven't been able to figure out from my reading
> and wiz mode. I don't mind spoilers; the game is challenging enough. :-)
> 
> 1. Are there any monsters that will loot my stash if I don't lock it? Any
> advantage to using a chest vs. a large box?

No, the containers are not looted by creatures. Though some containers
(sacks, bags) may be eaten by certain monsters. I don't know of any
advantage between the two object types; if there is one it was never of
any relevance in my games.

> 2. Prayer timeout: I've seen lots of references that there is one, but
> how do you calculate the actual value?

It's a few hundreds of turns, but they say that the value is not fixed
and the formula not simple to calculate.

> 3. Tossing food at monsters (ponies/horses/etc.) can make them "peaceful"
> but is it possible to make them "tame" on food alone, or do you need
> charm monster/taming magic?

Some monsters are tamable by food. Dogs and cats if you toss some tripe
rations, horses like vegetables, for example. Other monsters require the
magic spells, but these spells are not qually successful on monsters.

> 4. Rusted/burnt and corroded/rotted: Are these "erosion" damages somehow
> paired the way I've listed them here? I know about reading enchant
> armor/weapon while confused, and that !oOil restores rusted and corroded
> items... any other way to restore burnt and rotted items?

No other way. Mind that oil will just (temporarily) fix weapons, and only
one level of rust.

You may also like to make your armor rust/rot/erode-proof by reading a
cursed scroll of destroy armor while being confused. But this method is
only usable as prophylaxis and won't fix already damaged armor.

> 5. I've seen references to naming one's weapons Sting or Orcrist; what
> does that do? (I use daggers.)

Hmm.., personally I won't do that. It is possible to create these two
artifact weapons by simply naming a weapon of the respective base weapon
type, an elven dagger and an elven broadsword. But these two weapons are
quite the worst of all existing artifacts. And every existing artifact
will make it less probable to get better ones. I'd rather go to an altar
and sacrifice corpses to get a more effective artifact weapon from your
god.

> 6. I know some of the dangers of turn-to-stone corpses. *trice corpses
> (even blessed ones) rot away over time; any way to preserve them? Was
> I imagining it, or did a *trice corpse I put in an oilskin sack later
> turn into a copper spellbook?

An ice box will preserve the corpses. A corpse won't turn into a spellbook;
but if you drank a potion of hallucination you might get such impressions.

